    explanation: "By default, SQLAlchemy relationships are lazily loaded, meaning accessing order.items for each order in a loop issues a separate SELECT per order rather than one combined query. This is easy to miss in development with small datasets but becomes a severe performance problem in production with realistic data volumes, and it's especially dangerous in async code where each lazy load can raise a MissingGreenlet error instead of quietly firing a query. The fix is eager loading: selectinload issues one extra bulk query for all related rows, while joinedload uses a SQL JOIN, both replacing N queries with a small constant number.",
    code: "from sqlalchemy.orm import selectinload\nfrom sqlalchemy import select\n\n# N+1: one query for orders, then one query per order for items\norders = db.scalars(select(Order)).all()\nfor order in orders:\n    print(order.items)  # triggers a query each iteration\n\n# fixed: eager load items in a single additional query\nstmt = select(Order).options(selectinload(Order.items))\norders = db.scalars(stmt).all()\n",
